ervice_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-28T22:49:32.268Z", "request_id": "req_011CgVT7ztEJNwboTrT1oaTU", "thinking_duration_ms": 27688}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgVT816PA61Z7sUom87ou", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I judged these by reasoning through the rules approximately, not by simulating them step by step, so treat the probabilities as rough.\n\n**What drives the result**\n- **Running short costs far more than over-ordering.** For good 1, each unit of unmet demand loses 7 of margin plus a penalty of 3, about 10 in total. For good 0 it is 4 + 2 = 6. An extra unit costs only 0.7–0.8 per day in storage. With a shelf life of 8 days and orders every 1–2 days, spoilage is rare. So orders at about 1.15–1.3 times the forecast should beat orders at the forecast.\n- **The average demand multiplier is 1.046, not 1.** A plan that orders exactly the forecast therefore runs short regularly.\n- **Daily versus every-other-day ordering.** Ordering every day costs about 312 more in order fees. Ordering every other day costs roughly 480 more in storage, because one day's demand sits overnight. These roughly cancel, with a slight edge to daily ordering.\n- **Warehouse capacity (188) doesn't bind for the 2-day plans**, even at 1.3 times the forecast: an arrival plus leftovers comes to about 155.\n- **Days 0–2 are short for every plan.** The starting stock (28 and 42) doesn't cover them, so this doesn't separate the candidates.\n\n**By candidate**\n- **7:** orders ev